    "body": "Well the common use case is where the element is removed and created from scratch from a template using a framework, e.g. React's JSX. [Here is a simple example of that](https://codesandbox.io/p/sandbox/adoring-flower-fhpgp8?file=%2Fsrc%2Findex.js&workspaceId=58e84dfa-e2dc-4262-a4f5-95726e9a9473).\r\nWhen you switch tabs the text below gets wrapped with a custom element only on \"Pane 2\", where it gets a border and becomes \"clickable\". Clicking it alternates a custom state.\r\nIf you click it so it becomes \"checked\" (gets a salmon background-color), then switch to \"Pane 1\" and back again, it will lose this state.\r\n\r\nThe point is that code like this gets written a lot, and that is what many frameworks do.\r\nThe goal is to improve the interoperability of Custom Elements and frameworks, so that managing state in each doesn't interfere with the other, and by that remove friction.",
